WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.667 --> 00:00:03.667
-It is an honor to meet you.
I really love that you're here.

00:00:03.667 --> 00:00:05.367
Thank you so much.
-Oh, thank you for having me.

00:00:05.367 --> 00:00:07.400
I love being here.

00:00:07.400 --> 00:00:09.534
-I could not sing "Wannabe"
in front of you.

00:00:09.534 --> 00:00:10.767
That's embarrassing.

00:00:10.767 --> 00:00:12.701
I -- Oh, my gosh.
I can't do it front of you.

00:00:12.701 --> 00:00:15.834
But I'm happy you're here.
I met your husband, by the way.

00:00:15.834 --> 00:00:18.334
-You did. He told me about you.
-Ooh.

00:00:18.334 --> 00:00:19.667
-He did. No, he had
a really good time with you.

00:00:19.667 --> 00:00:20.901
-Okay, good. Yeah.

00:00:20.901 --> 00:00:22.167
-You were doing
the racing together.

00:00:22.167 --> 00:00:23.567
-We were racing go-karts
together.

00:00:23.567 --> 00:00:26.067
Yeah, Christian Horner,
who's, like --

00:00:26.067 --> 00:00:27.767
Was it Oracle? Red Bull?

00:00:27.767 --> 00:00:29.501
-Yeah. Red Bull.
He's the team principal.

00:00:29.501 --> 00:00:31.467
-F1. F1. He's massive.

00:00:31.467 --> 00:00:33.067
And then I found out
he's married to you,

00:00:33.067 --> 00:00:34.434
and I go, "Oh, please."

00:00:34.434 --> 00:00:35.667
[ Laughter ]

00:00:35.667 --> 00:00:37.300
Chris -- He can come back
any time you want.

00:00:37.300 --> 00:00:39.167
But, you? Come on!
-Oh, thank you very much.

00:00:39.167 --> 00:00:40.367
-Welcome to "The Tonight Show."

00:00:40.367 --> 00:00:42.434
[ Cheers and applause ]
-Thank you.

00:00:42.434 --> 00:00:45.100
He brought back -- Do you know
how when you go on a TV show,

00:00:45.100 --> 00:00:46.701
they give you
a little freebie gifts?

00:00:46.701 --> 00:00:48.901
-Yeah.
-And he brought back a beach bag

00:00:48.901 --> 00:00:50.434
with your name on it,
and I kept it

00:00:50.434 --> 00:00:51.934
and took you on holiday with me.

00:00:51.934 --> 00:00:53.133
-No way!
-Yeah, I did.

00:00:53.133 --> 00:00:55.234
-I'm honored!
Oh, I have to get you some swag.

00:00:55.234 --> 00:00:57.934
I'm gonna get you some stuff.
-It was very practical.

00:00:57.934 --> 00:00:59.868
-I got a nice gift
from you today, by the way.

00:00:59.868 --> 00:01:02.634
I don't want -- But you got me
some silky pajamas.

00:01:02.634 --> 00:01:04.601
I was like...
-Do you know, I was thinking.

00:01:04.601 --> 00:01:07.067
Rather than -- You know, we can
all buy each other flowers

00:01:07.067 --> 00:01:08.467
and, you know, generic things.

00:01:08.467 --> 00:01:11.133
So I was thinking,
you know, this man, to me,

00:01:11.133 --> 00:01:13.767
intuitively, you look sort of
quite a dapper gentleman...

00:01:13.767 --> 00:01:15.067
-Thank you.

00:01:15.067 --> 00:01:17.033
-...and, you know,
quite old-school.

00:01:17.033 --> 00:01:18.367
-Yeah, that's exactly right.
-Yeah?

00:01:18.367 --> 00:01:19.634
-Yes.
-And I thought, you know,

00:01:19.634 --> 00:01:21.934
give you something
you can really appreciate,

00:01:21.934 --> 00:01:23.567
you know,
move around the house...

00:01:23.567 --> 00:01:26.901
-Finally someone knows me.
And you are the one.

00:01:26.901 --> 00:01:29.767
-You've done really well
in your life. Congratulations.

00:01:29.767 --> 00:01:31.400
Can I just tell everyone --

00:01:31.400 --> 00:01:34.868
a long time ago
I went on "Jay Leno," so...

00:01:34.868 --> 00:01:36.167
-Yeah.
-And you filled those shoes.

00:01:36.167 --> 00:01:38.267
-That's right. Exactly.
-Big shoes to fill.

00:01:38.267 --> 00:01:40.634
And you've done incredibly well.
-Oh, stop. No.

00:01:40.634 --> 00:01:42.634
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:01:42.634 --> 00:01:46.400
-Because when you're the boss --
When you're the boss --

00:01:46.400 --> 00:01:48.300
When you're the boss,
nobody says, "Well done,"

00:01:48.300 --> 00:01:49.701
because they think
you've got it.

00:01:49.701 --> 00:01:52.501
So well done.
Your mother is very proud.

00:01:52.501 --> 00:01:53.701
-[ Laughs ]
-Yes.

00:01:53.701 --> 00:01:54.901
-Thank you so much.

00:01:54.901 --> 00:01:57.234
-You come from a small town,
very rural.

00:01:57.234 --> 00:01:58.934
Was it Saugerties?

00:01:58.934 --> 00:02:00.467
-Yes.
How do you know all that?

00:02:00.467 --> 00:02:03.133
-Yeah, so you are inspirational
for all those kids out there

00:02:03.133 --> 00:02:05.067
that look
for the American dream.

00:02:05.067 --> 00:02:07.067
You are it.
Everyone -- You know.

00:02:07.067 --> 00:02:08.601
We're all so proud, Jimmy.

00:02:08.601 --> 00:02:11.701
-Good night, everybody.
That's it.

00:02:11.701 --> 00:02:13.834
We have things to talk about.
I want to talk about you.

00:02:13.834 --> 00:02:16.567
Don't embarrass me anymore.
I want to talk about you.

00:02:16.567 --> 00:02:20.467
I want to talk about the book.
Is this a -- Can I ask what --

00:02:20.467 --> 00:02:22.200
Because your book tour
started in New York.

00:02:22.200 --> 00:02:24.167
What are your memories
of New York?

00:02:24.167 --> 00:02:26.167
Was it a Spice Girl? Was that
the first time you were here?

00:02:26.167 --> 00:02:29.667
-Yeah, I mean, I'd seen
New York in a lot of movies,

00:02:29.667 --> 00:02:33.400
and, you know,
I love those old classics.

00:02:33.400 --> 00:02:34.801
They brought me up.

00:02:34.801 --> 00:02:36.067
You know,
when my mother was at work,

00:02:36.067 --> 00:02:37.701
I'd see all these movies,
and they --

00:02:37.701 --> 00:02:39.100
they sold me the American dream.

00:02:39.100 --> 00:02:42.400
I don't think I would be a
success as I was and I have been

00:02:42.400 --> 00:02:43.834
if it wasn't for America,

00:02:43.834 --> 00:02:45.534
because you say,
"You can do it."

00:02:45.534 --> 00:02:48.334
And so when I got here,
I was like,

00:02:48.334 --> 00:02:50.467
"Wow! Look at these skyscrapers.

00:02:50.467 --> 00:02:53.567
These yellow taxis.
This is amazing."

00:02:53.567 --> 00:02:55.534
-I was wondering, if we --
If you don't mind,

00:02:55.534 --> 00:02:57.434
if I can ask you
some Spice Girls rumors...

00:02:57.434 --> 00:02:58.534
-Yeah, of course.

00:02:58.534 --> 00:02:59.734
-...and you tell me
if they're true.

00:02:59.734 --> 00:03:00.767
-Yeah.
-Okay. Really?

00:03:00.767 --> 00:03:02.067
-Yeah, yeah, absolutely,
of course.

00:03:02.067 --> 00:03:03.934
-Is it true that you've missed
the initial audition

00:03:03.934 --> 00:03:06.501
for the Spice Girls?
-That is true.

00:03:06.501 --> 00:03:07.901
And I'd like to say
it wasn't really

00:03:07.901 --> 00:03:09.467
the Spice Girls at that moment.

00:03:09.467 --> 00:03:12.601
It was just some people
auditioning for a girl band.

00:03:12.601 --> 00:03:14.067
I did miss it.

00:03:14.067 --> 00:03:16.601
I was up a mountain
because my mother's Spanish

00:03:16.601 --> 00:03:18.667
and I'd burnt my face.

00:03:18.667 --> 00:03:22.067
Like, it was like really bad,
like an apple and...

00:03:22.067 --> 00:03:24.267
-Just from sun?
The sun beating down on you?

00:03:24.267 --> 00:03:26.501
-Ski -- In the Pyrenees.
-Oh.

00:03:26.501 --> 00:03:28.033
-Yeah, so I was up a mountain,

00:03:28.033 --> 00:03:30.767
and I didn't realize
you had to put sunscreen on

00:03:30.767 --> 00:03:33.334
up a mountain --
because it was snowing.

00:03:33.334 --> 00:03:35.767
And that was back
in, like, the 90s.

00:03:35.767 --> 00:03:38.501
You thought, "Oh, yeah. I'm
gonna get the suntan," and...

00:03:38.501 --> 00:03:40.667
-No, you have to dress
like Taraji P. Henson

00:03:40.667 --> 00:03:42.200
going to a Beyoncé concert.

00:03:42.200 --> 00:03:44.300
That's how you dress when you go
skiing. You cover everything up.

00:03:44.300 --> 00:03:47.300
-Okay.
So I didn't go to the audition.

00:03:47.300 --> 00:03:50.133
But somehow I just kept
that advert on my mirror,

00:03:50.133 --> 00:03:51.934
and a few months later,
I thought,

00:03:51.934 --> 00:03:53.934
"Hmm. I wonder
if that's still going."

00:03:53.934 --> 00:03:56.501
So I just rang them up
and they said, "Come along."

00:03:56.501 --> 00:03:58.234
-The rest is history.
Number two --

00:03:58.234 --> 00:04:00.367
Is it true
that we have you to thank

00:04:00.367 --> 00:04:02.434
for the name "Spice Girls"?

00:04:02.434 --> 00:04:07.667
-Well, so, I was in a --
I was in an exercise class.

00:04:07.667 --> 00:04:10.434
And back in the '90s and '80s,
you did the grapevine.

00:04:10.434 --> 00:04:12.534
Do you remember that
in aerobics?

00:04:12.534 --> 00:04:14.067
So I was doing that.
-No, yeah, no.

00:04:14.067 --> 00:04:17.367
-So I get a lot of my ideas --
-What's the grapevine?

00:04:17.367 --> 00:04:19.234
-You know when you do --
The '90s.

00:04:19.234 --> 00:04:21.000
And you have to go step
like that.

00:04:21.000 --> 00:04:22.701
-Oh, like --
Oh, a step class. Oh, yeah.

00:04:22.701 --> 00:04:24.734
-That kind of thing. Exactly.
-Yeah, yeah. Anyway --

00:04:24.734 --> 00:04:27.033
So, anyway --
So I was doing that.

00:04:27.033 --> 00:04:28.868
And sometimes when I'm
doing exercise, you get ideas.

00:04:28.868 --> 00:04:30.100
-Yeah.

00:04:30.100 --> 00:04:31.567
-And I thought,
"We're all different.

00:04:31.567 --> 00:04:32.834
What about Spice?"

00:04:32.834 --> 00:04:35.501
So I ran back home
and I called up the stairs

00:04:35.501 --> 00:04:37.300
to all the other girls and said,
"What about Spice?"

00:04:37.300 --> 00:04:39.167
And they were like, "Yeah!"
-Really?

00:04:39.167 --> 00:04:40.767
-Yeah!

00:04:40.767 --> 00:04:42.534
-But what were you
originally called?

00:04:42.534 --> 00:04:45.601
-Okay, This is when we weren't
fully formed as a group,

00:04:45.601 --> 00:04:49.267
but the management at the time,
they called us Touch,

00:04:49.267 --> 00:04:50.634
but they got...

00:04:50.634 --> 00:04:51.968
-Yeah. So --
-Yeah.

00:04:51.968 --> 00:04:54.033
-So they got rid of
the management and Touch.

00:04:54.033 --> 00:04:56.167
-Yeah.
-"We're not Touch."

00:04:56.167 --> 00:04:58.200
-Exactly.
-That would've been a disaster.

00:04:58.200 --> 00:05:00.400
-No touching.
-No touching at all, no.

00:05:00.400 --> 00:05:01.767
Spice Girls.
-Okay.

00:05:01.767 --> 00:05:04.267
-Yeah. And did you go, "Ginger"?

00:05:04.267 --> 00:05:07.634
-Well, that was just made up.
You know, the tag name.

00:05:07.634 --> 00:05:09.100
Some magazine made it up, but...

00:05:09.100 --> 00:05:10.567
-Really?
-Yeah.

00:05:10.567 --> 00:05:13.133
-I just love this stuff.
Okay, great.

00:05:13.133 --> 00:05:16.601
As Ginger Spice, you became
known for the Union Jack dress.

00:05:16.601 --> 00:05:18.100
-Yes.
-And I have a photo of it.

00:05:18.100 --> 00:05:19.300
-Mm-hmm.
-Come on.

00:05:19.300 --> 00:05:21.701
Are you kidding me?
How cool is this?

00:05:21.701 --> 00:05:24.467
Now, did you --
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:05:24.467 --> 00:05:26.601
Is it true that
you designed this dress?

00:05:26.601 --> 00:05:28.300
-Yeah, 'cause
what happened was --

00:05:28.300 --> 00:05:30.167
So I always like making things.

00:05:30.167 --> 00:05:33.868
And, uh, they gave us,
like, different clothes

00:05:33.868 --> 00:05:35.467
from different --
That was a Gucci dress.

00:05:35.467 --> 00:05:37.934
It was, like, a black
Marilyn Monroe swimming costume,

00:05:37.934 --> 00:05:39.567
like '50s shape.
-Yeah.

00:05:39.567 --> 00:05:41.434
-And I was thinking --
Like Americans,

00:05:41.434 --> 00:05:43.133
you know how
you always celebrate,

00:05:43.133 --> 00:05:44.667
you know, your country,
and I was thinking,

00:05:44.667 --> 00:05:47.033
"it's the Brits.
I should put a flag on it."

00:05:47.033 --> 00:05:49.634
And so the only way I could
find a flag was a tea towel.

00:05:49.634 --> 00:05:53.334
So I cut out a tea towel
and sewed it on the Gucci dress.

00:05:53.334 --> 00:05:54.601
-You're kidding me?
-No.

00:05:54.601 --> 00:05:56.100
-And performed at the Brits?

00:05:56.100 --> 00:05:59.467
-Yeah, and then those boots,
they're with car spray.

00:05:59.467 --> 00:06:02.000
'Cause my father was a mechanic.

00:06:02.000 --> 00:06:03.801
And -- Yeah, car spray.

00:06:03.801 --> 00:06:06.000
-That's car spray?
-Yeah.

00:06:06.000 --> 00:06:07.300
-This --
Is this the greatest story?

00:06:07.300 --> 00:06:09.868
Come on. Thank you for --

00:06:09.868 --> 00:06:11.334
[ Cheers and applause ]
-Okay.

00:06:11.334 --> 00:06:14.934
-Alright. So, you've done that.
You've kind of done it all.

00:06:14.934 --> 00:06:17.167
-You're very creative, though.
I was watching you.

00:06:17.167 --> 00:06:18.434
Can I just say --

00:06:18.434 --> 00:06:20.634
I didn't realize this
until I saw your skit.

00:06:20.634 --> 00:06:22.834
You are so funny.

00:06:22.834 --> 00:06:24.400
[ Laughter ]

00:06:24.400 --> 00:06:26.100
You are really good.
And you wrote that.

00:06:26.100 --> 00:06:27.834
You wrote it with your wife,
didn't you?

00:06:27.834 --> 00:06:30.200
It was very, very good.
It was very good.

00:06:30.200 --> 00:06:32.534
-You felt that?
-Yeah. It was very good.

00:06:32.534 --> 00:06:34.000
-I do run things by my wife.

00:06:34.000 --> 00:06:35.467
She doesn't get any credit
for anything.

00:06:35.467 --> 00:06:38.300
-But don't we all run things
by our partners?

00:06:38.300 --> 00:06:39.968
-Did your husband
help with this?

00:06:39.968 --> 00:06:42.267
-Well, okay, so I said --
[ Laughter ]

00:06:42.267 --> 00:06:45.033
No, no. Okay. Mnh. Right.

00:06:45.033 --> 00:06:47.367
Anyone can identify with this,
I'm sure, right?

00:06:47.367 --> 00:06:49.234
So I said, "You know, darling,
I'm writing this book."

00:06:49.234 --> 00:06:50.601
And he was like, "Yeah, yeah."

00:06:50.601 --> 00:06:52.267
This was about nine years ago
I started.

00:06:52.267 --> 00:06:53.667
-Nine years ago?

00:06:53.667 --> 00:06:55.767
-Yeah, by the time it's come
to the shelves, okay?

00:06:55.767 --> 00:06:58.601
And I've written books before,
but this one's a chunky one.

00:06:58.601 --> 00:07:02.133
And I keep on going off to my
writing shed and disappearing.

00:07:02.133 --> 00:07:04.400
And then I would say, "Oh,
can I -- Would you read it?"

00:07:04.400 --> 00:07:05.901
You know,
different drafts of it.

00:07:05.901 --> 00:07:07.968
And he was like, "I'll wait
till it's published."

00:07:07.968 --> 00:07:09.467
That's what he kept on
saying to me.

00:07:09.467 --> 00:07:11.033
-Yeah. Good for him.

00:07:11.033 --> 00:07:13.300
-Anyway, seven years later,
finally, I go,

00:07:13.300 --> 00:07:14.868
"Read it! It's coming out."

00:07:14.868 --> 00:07:17.200
-"Hey. Hey. Read it, buddy.
Figure it out."

00:07:17.200 --> 00:07:20.100
-And the first thing
he says to me, he goes...

00:07:20.100 --> 00:07:22.934
"Ooh. It's better
than I thought."

00:07:22.934 --> 00:07:24.934
[ Laughter ]
[ Audience ohhs ]

00:07:24.934 --> 00:07:26.000
I was like, "Ah!"
-Ooh.

00:07:26.000 --> 00:07:27.834
-"Ooh," right?
-Off to the writing shed.

00:07:27.834 --> 00:07:29.133
That's what I say. Christian...

00:07:29.133 --> 00:07:32.467
-And then he got to the end,
and he went,

00:07:32.467 --> 00:07:35.300
"Do you know what?
I couldn't put it down."

00:07:35.300 --> 00:07:36.801
He said, "I just wanted

00:07:36.801 --> 00:07:38.667
to turn the page
to find out what happened next."

00:07:38.667 --> 00:07:40.400
I was like, "My work is done."

00:07:40.400 --> 00:07:41.901
-[ Laughs ] Yeah.

00:07:41.901 --> 00:07:44.400
Because who is --
-Okay.

00:07:44.400 --> 00:07:47.501
-Who is it for and what is it?
What can we say it's about?

00:07:47.501 --> 00:07:49.033
-Okay.
So first and foremost,

00:07:49.033 --> 00:07:50.868
I think the world
needs a new hero.

00:07:50.868 --> 00:07:52.300
An ordinary hero.

00:07:52.300 --> 00:07:54.133
Someone that's not airbrushed,

00:07:54.133 --> 00:07:56.000
that, you know,
finds a courage

00:07:56.000 --> 00:07:57.767
you never knew you had,
vulnerable.

00:07:57.767 --> 00:07:58.834
-Yes!
-Okay.

00:07:58.834 --> 00:08:02.434
-So, it's based on an island,
okay,

00:08:02.434 --> 00:08:04.701
that's a bit like --
I can't say the movie,

00:08:04.701 --> 00:08:07.467
but it's a bit like...
-Oh, I know.

00:08:07.467 --> 00:08:09.834
-Dinosaurs,
but instead of dinosaurs,

00:08:09.834 --> 00:08:12.200
it's endangered animals, okay?

00:08:12.200 --> 00:08:14.767
-Like almost like a --
-Yeah.

00:08:14.767 --> 00:08:16.601
-It's like a park.
-Yeah.

00:08:16.601 --> 00:08:19.334
-So, anyway --
Anyway, so, they're --

00:08:19.334 --> 00:08:21.100
The school --
Okay, you'll like this.

00:08:21.100 --> 00:08:23.334
Because I think you would have
gone to this school, right?

00:08:23.334 --> 00:08:27.400
So this school was built
500 years ago, right?

00:08:27.400 --> 00:08:30.467
And it was built
by Queen Elizabeth I

00:08:30.467 --> 00:08:33.367
in honor of her mother,
Anne Boleyn, okay,

00:08:33.367 --> 00:08:36.200
Who got her head chopped off
by her husband.

00:08:36.200 --> 00:08:37.667
Not nice, right?
-Yes.

00:08:37.667 --> 00:08:41.000
-So Queen Elizabeth I
builds the school, and she says,

00:08:41.000 --> 00:08:43.234
"Do you know what?
I'm not gonna get married.

00:08:43.234 --> 00:08:45.367
I'm off that.
But instead of the heirs,

00:08:45.367 --> 00:08:47.267
I'm gonna have the students,
polymaths...

00:08:47.267 --> 00:08:48.868
really smart people."

00:08:48.868 --> 00:08:51.701
I think you might have
gone there, right?

00:08:51.701 --> 00:08:53.167
"They're gonna be my legacy."

00:08:53.167 --> 00:08:54.400
Okay?
-Yeah.

00:08:54.400 --> 00:08:56.734
-500 years later, Rosie Frost.

00:08:56.734 --> 00:08:58.934
And she's, like, from
the back end of nowhere,

00:08:58.934 --> 00:09:00.267
like, back end of London.

00:09:00.267 --> 00:09:02.000
She gets sent there, and
she's like, "Why am I here?"

00:09:02.000 --> 00:09:03.200
She's lost her mother.

00:09:03.200 --> 00:09:05.300
Anyway,
and she enters this game.

